es on all three interfaces (never touching the pre-existing LAN/SSH pass rule), and after diagnosing that pf states pin routing decisions to the rule that created them, gave opt1/opt2 their own upstream gateways so OPNsense auto-applies reply-to (return traffic exits the same transit network it arrived on)","Configured node3 and node4 via persistent NetworkManager connections (nmcli) with static IPs on their second NICs and static routes to each other's subnet across the router pair","Fixed a duplicate-IP misconfiguration (node4 and node2 both on 10.10.22.1) discovered via failed ARP resolution","Discovered and fixed a RHEL9 firewalld default-zone gap: cockpit's built-in service rule incidentally allowed TCP/9090 but not TCP/8080, causing the first steering test to fail asymmetrically; opened both ports explicitly and persistently via firewall-cmd --permanent","Verified with simultaneous tcpdump captures on node1 eth1/eth2 during real curl HTTP requests from node3 to node4 that the entire TCP/8080 flow (SYN through FIN, both directions) stays exclusively on Transit Network 1 and the entire TCP/9090 flow stays exclusively on Transit Network 2","Captured healthy boot-identity and service-health baselines on all four nodes, then sequentially restarted node1, node2, node3, and node4 one at a time (VyOS/RHEL via `reboot`, OPNsense via `/sbin/reboot`), polling exec availability, proving changed boot_id/kern.boottime on each, and confirming full recovery of addressing, routing, firewall rules, and end-to-end port-based traffic steering after each restart","Restarted the ad-hoc HTTP listeners on node4 after its reboot (not persisted by design, unlike all network/firewall config) and re-verified full end-to-end steering behavior after all four restarts"],"evidence":[{"requirement":"Steer node3->node4 TCP/8080 traffic across the first transit network","command_ids":["cmd-355e40f251a2407d","cmd-d8e0550181fc490d","cmd-10e2501e945c4e59"],"summary":"VyOS PBR rule 10 (dest port 80